1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,117
This video explains my
investment report on Visa,

2
00:00:03,117 --> 00:00:06,806
the card network, ticker V on
the New York Stock Exchange.

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:10,203
The analysis date is 17 October 2026.

4
00:00:10,203 --> 00:00:15,319
It explains an analysis; it is
not personal financial advice.

5
00:00:16,133 --> 00:00:20,260
Here is the question. You live
in Norway, you invest in kroner,

6
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:23,404
and you are thinking of
putting 30,000 to 50,000

7
00:00:23,404 --> 00:00:25,762
kroner into Visa for 10 to 20 years,

8
00:00:25,762 --> 00:00:28,382
instead of a low-cost global index fund.

9
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:31,847
So the question is not whether
Visa is a good company.

10
00:00:31,847 --> 00:00:35,751
It is whether Visa, at today's
price, beats the index fund for you.

11
00:00:36,567 --> 00:00:38,778
The bottom line first: hold, or wait.

12
00:00:39,067 --> 00:00:47,446
Don't buy at today's $402.61. Become
a buyer at or below about $345.

13
00:00:48,133 --> 00:00:53,014
Visa is an exceptional business, but
at this price its expected return,

14
00:00:53,014 --> 00:00:56,726
about 8.5% a year, is no
better than the index fund's,

15
00:00:56,726 --> 00:00:59,064
and a single stock is far riskier.

16
00:00:59,867 --> 00:01:03,858
The video has three parts: is this
a great business, what is it worth,

17
00:01:03,858 --> 00:01:07,791
and how does it compare with the
index fund for a Norwegian investor?

18
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:12,885
I'll lean on maths you know from
engineering, like exponentials, series,

19
00:01:12,885 --> 00:01:17,884
derivatives and root finding, and define
each finance concept when it first appears.

20
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:21,511
As in the report, I separate
facts from cited sources,

21
00:01:21,511 --> 00:01:26,416
estimates from my Python models, and my
own judgments, with three coloured tags.

22
00:01:27,933 --> 00:01:29,933
Chapter one. How Visa makes money.

23
00:01:31,333 --> 00:01:34,696
When you tap a Visa card in a
shop, four parties are involved.

24
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,827
You, the cardholder. The merchant.

25
00:01:36,827 --> 00:01:39,030
The merchant's bank, called the acquirer.

26
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:41,771
And your bank, the issuer,
which gave you the card.

27
00:01:42,067 --> 00:01:44,625
Visa's network, VisaNet,
sits in the middle.

28
00:01:44,625 --> 00:01:47,242
It carries the message
between the two banks,

29
00:01:47,242 --> 00:01:51,022
gets the payment approved, and
then clears and settles the money.

30
00:01:51,333 --> 00:01:53,829
The merchant pays a fee on every purchase.

31
00:01:53,829 --> 00:01:57,632
Most of it, the interchange fee,
goes to your bank, not to Visa.

32
00:01:57,933 --> 00:02:01,179
Visa charges its own, much
smaller fees to both banks,

33
00:02:01,179 --> 00:02:05,746
and pays part of them back as client
incentives, to win the banks' business.

34
00:02:06,533 --> 00:02:10,407
In fiscal 2025, the year to 30 September,

35
00:02:10,407 --> 00:02:15,320
that gave gross revenue of
$55.8bn, from four lines.

36
00:02:15,633 --> 00:02:18,418
Service revenue is
charged on payment volume,

37
00:02:18,418 --> 00:02:21,389
and data processing revenue
on each transaction.

38
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:23,824
International transaction revenue,

39
00:02:23,824 --> 00:02:27,011
from cross-border payments
and currency conversion,

40
00:02:27,011 --> 00:02:29,885
is the most profitable
and most cyclical line.

41
00:02:29,885 --> 00:02:33,510
Other revenue includes licensing
and value-added services.

42
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:39,121
The client incentives of $15.8bn
are subtracted as contra revenue,

43
00:02:39,121 --> 00:02:44,362
which means negative revenue. That
leaves net revenue of $40.0bn.

44
00:02:45,167 --> 00:02:49,191
An engineer's model of this business:
revenue is traffic times a toll.

45
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:54,797
Visa processed 257.5 billion
transactions in fiscal 2025,

46
00:02:54,797 --> 00:03:01,024
which works out at about 15.5 cents
of net revenue per transaction.

47
00:03:01,333 --> 00:03:05,860
Since 2016 the number of
transactions has more than tripled,

48
00:03:05,860 --> 00:03:09,933
while the toll has stayed
between 14.6 and 18.1 cents.

49
00:03:10,733 --> 00:03:14,728
Notice what Visa is not: a lender.
Your bank extends the credit,

50
00:03:14,728 --> 00:03:18,410
sets the interest rate and
takes the loss if you don't pay.

51
00:03:18,410 --> 00:03:21,094
Visa has no loan book
and no credit losses.

52
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:24,621
Its one balance-sheet risk is settlement:

53
00:03:24,621 --> 00:03:29,098
it guarantees payments between
banks if one fails to pay.

54
00:03:29,098 --> 00:03:33,183
The peak daily exposure in
fiscal 2025 was $153.4bn,

55
00:03:33,183 --> 00:03:37,581
but Visa says its losses have
historically been minimal.

56
00:03:39,097 --> 00:03:41,739
Chapter two. Why the
economics are exceptional.

57
00:03:43,131 --> 00:03:45,100
Why can Visa charge a toll at all?

58
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:47,301
Because of a two-sided network effect.

59
00:03:47,597 --> 00:03:50,684
Cardholders want a card
that is accepted everywhere,

60
00:03:50,684 --> 00:03:53,888
and merchants accept the
cards that cardholders carry.

61
00:03:53,888 --> 00:03:56,322
A newcomer has to win both sides at once.

62
00:03:56,631 --> 00:04:01,037
That's why the same few networks
have dominated for decades.

63
00:04:01,037 --> 00:04:06,544
In 2025, Visa handled 40.8% of all card
purchase transactions in the world.

64
00:04:07,331 --> 00:04:09,906
The second reason is operating leverage.

65
00:04:09,906 --> 00:04:14,090
Most of Visa's costs are fixed:
people, technology and marketing.

66
00:04:14,090 --> 00:04:16,794
One more transaction costs almost nothing.

67
00:04:17,097 --> 00:04:21,206
Write the costs as a fixed
part F, plus v per transaction.

68
00:04:21,206 --> 00:04:24,748
With revenue p per transaction
and Q transactions,

69
00:04:24,748 --> 00:04:29,141
the operating margin is one,
minus v over p, minus F over p Q.

70
00:04:29,431 --> 00:04:33,425
As Q grows, the fixed-cost
term falls like one over Q,

71
00:04:33,425 --> 00:04:36,458
and the margin climbs toward its ceiling.

72
00:04:36,458 --> 00:04:42,154
Visa's network and processing costs were
just 2.2% of revenue in fiscal 2025.

73
00:04:42,964 --> 00:04:46,672
The operating margin is operating
profit divided by revenue:

74
00:04:46,672 --> 00:04:50,628
what's left after running the
business, before interest and tax.

75
00:04:50,931 --> 00:04:54,893
Under GAAP, the official
US accounting rules,

76
00:04:54,893 --> 00:05:01,233
Visa's was 60.0% in fiscal 2025, a
year with a $2.6bn lawsuit provision.

77
00:05:01,233 --> 00:05:07,837
Management's adjusted, non-GAAP figures
leave such items out, giving 67.7%.

78
00:05:08,131 --> 00:05:11,138
That adjusted margin has
been flat for three years,

79
00:05:11,138 --> 00:05:15,088
so future profit growth must come
mainly from revenue and buybacks.

80
00:05:15,897 --> 00:05:19,970
The third measure is return
on invested capital, or ROIC.

81
00:05:19,970 --> 00:05:22,471
Think of the business as a machine:

82
00:05:22,471 --> 00:05:27,686
ROIC is the after-tax operating profit
per dollar of capital put into it.

83
00:05:27,997 --> 00:05:31,404
The providers of that
capital expect a return,

84
00:05:31,404 --> 00:05:35,106
the cost of capital, which
for Visa is about 8.5%.

85
00:05:35,106 --> 00:05:37,402
I'll derive it in chapter five.

86
00:05:37,402 --> 00:05:41,474
Value is created when ROIC
exceeds the cost of capital.

87
00:05:42,364 --> 00:05:52,552
Visa's ROIC rose from 21% in fiscal 2015
to 32% in 2025, and to 46% net of its cash.

88
00:05:53,364 --> 00:05:55,575
Even that understates it.

89
00:05:55,575 --> 00:06:01,767
$47.5bn of Visa's assets are goodwill
and intangibles from past deals,

90
00:06:01,767 --> 00:06:06,454
more than its entire net
invested capital of $43.1bn.

91
00:06:06,454 --> 00:06:09,373
Its tangible capital is negative.

92
00:06:09,664 --> 00:06:12,842
Capital expenditure was
only 3.7% of revenue.

93
00:06:12,842 --> 00:06:15,738
Visa needs almost no new capital to grow,

94
00:06:15,738 --> 00:06:19,199
which is why nearly all
its cash can be paid out.

95
00:06:20,697 --> 00:06:22,875
Chapter three. Growth and compounding.

96
00:06:24,264 --> 00:06:32,203
Net revenue grew from $13.9bn in
fiscal 2015 to $40.0bn in 2025.

97
00:06:32,497 --> 00:06:36,976
Finance summarises this with the
compound annual growth rate, or CAGR:

98
00:06:36,976 --> 00:06:41,071
the constant yearly rate that
connects the start and end values.

99
00:06:41,071 --> 00:06:45,167
It's the geometric mean of the
yearly growth factors, minus one.

100
00:06:45,464 --> 00:06:50,154
Here: 40.0 divided by 13.88,
to the power one tenth,

101
00:06:50,154 --> 00:06:53,131
minus one, which is 11.2% a year.

102
00:06:53,431 --> 00:06:57,232
On a log scale, compound growth
is a straight line whose slope

103
00:06:57,232 --> 00:06:59,624
is the log of one plus the growth rate.

104
00:06:59,624 --> 00:07:04,467
The doubling time is log two divided by
that slope: about six and a half years.

105
00:07:04,764 --> 00:07:09,948
Excluding the boost from the
2016 Visa Europe acquisition,

106
00:07:09,948 --> 00:07:14,327
growth was 10.2% a year
from fiscal 2017 to 2025,

107
00:07:14,327 --> 00:07:19,422
and it was 15.3% in the first
nine months of fiscal 2026.

108
00:07:20,231 --> 00:07:22,874
Shareholders care about profit per share.

109
00:07:22,874 --> 00:07:27,644
Earnings per share, or EPS, is net
income divided by the number of shares.

110
00:07:27,931 --> 00:07:31,666
Write it as revenue, times
net margin, divided by shares.

111
00:07:31,666 --> 00:07:36,581
Take logs and differentiate with respect
to time, and the growth rates add:

112
00:07:36,581 --> 00:07:41,758
EPS growth equals revenue growth, plus
margin growth, minus share-count growth.

113
00:07:42,064 --> 00:07:45,255
Visa's non-GAAP EPS grew 15.9% a year.

114
00:07:45,255 --> 00:07:49,119
In log terms, that's
10.6 points from revenue,

115
00:07:49,119 --> 00:07:53,906
2.0 from a wider margin, and
2.2 from buying back shares.

116
00:07:54,697 --> 00:07:58,408
One trend works against shareholders.

117
00:07:58,408 --> 00:08:06,730
Client incentives rose from 17.1% of gross
revenue in fiscal 2015 to 28.3% in 2025.

118
00:08:07,031 --> 00:08:11,542
Part of the 2019 step is an accounting
change, but the direction is clear:

119
00:08:11,542 --> 00:08:15,017
the big banks are capturing
a growing share of the value.

120
00:08:15,017 --> 00:08:17,151
It's one of the signals to monitor.

121
00:08:17,964 --> 00:08:21,181
In recessions, revenue has
held up remarkably well,

122
00:08:21,181 --> 00:08:23,956
because Visa earns on
spending, not lending.

123
00:08:24,264 --> 00:08:31,675
In fiscal 2009 it grew 10%. In 2020,
with travel shut down, it fell only 4.9%,

124
00:08:31,675 --> 00:08:35,285
although cross-border volume fell 22%.

125
00:08:35,597 --> 00:08:39,455
The share price is another matter.

126
00:08:39,455 --> 00:08:44,221
It fell 52% in 2008 and 2009, 36% in 2020,

127
00:08:44,221 --> 00:08:49,667
and 25% in 2022, a year
when revenue grew 21.6%.

128
00:08:49,964 --> 00:08:52,767
The business is far more
defensive than the stock.

129
00:08:52,767 --> 00:08:56,858
The price moves with what investors
will pay for each dollar of earnings,

130
00:08:56,858 --> 00:08:59,269
and that depends heavily
on interest rates.

131
00:09:00,764 --> 00:09:03,457
Chapter four. Free cash
flow, and where it goes.

132
00:09:04,864 --> 00:09:09,149
Accounting profit isn't cash. So
investors look at free cash flow:

133
00:09:09,149 --> 00:09:12,266
cash from operations,
minus capital expenditure.

134
00:09:12,564 --> 00:09:15,049
Draw a control volume around the company.

135
00:09:15,049 --> 00:09:18,018
Free cash flow is what
crosses the boundary after

136
00:09:18,018 --> 00:09:20,988
the business has paid to
run and maintain itself:

137
00:09:20,988 --> 00:09:23,230
the cash that could go to its owners.

138
00:09:24,031 --> 00:09:29,605
Visa's free cash flow was
$21.6bn in fiscal 2025,

139
00:09:29,605 --> 00:09:37,228
53.9% of revenue, and it has
compounded at 13.3% a year since 2015.

140
00:09:37,531 --> 00:09:42,951
The margin has drifted down, from
about 60% in fiscal 2021 to 2023,

141
00:09:42,951 --> 00:09:45,945
to 47.2% over the last twelve months.

142
00:09:45,945 --> 00:09:50,151
I couldn't pin down why,
so I treat it with caution.

143
00:09:50,964 --> 00:09:55,540
Part of Visa's staff pay is in shares,
called stock-based compensation.

144
00:09:55,540 --> 00:10:00,825
No cash leaves, but it dilutes existing
owners just like a cost, so I subtract it.

145
00:10:01,131 --> 00:10:05,780
Averaging the last three years
and the latest twelve months gives

146
00:10:05,780 --> 00:10:10,071
a normal free cash flow after
stock pay of 51.1% of revenue.

147
00:10:10,071 --> 00:10:12,717
That's the margin the valuation uses.

148
00:10:13,531 --> 00:10:20,030
Because Visa needs so little capital,
the cash goes back to shareholders:

149
00:10:20,030 --> 00:10:27,064
$138bn from fiscal 2015 to 2025, 94% of
its free cash flow, mostly as buybacks.

150
00:10:27,364 --> 00:10:29,151
A dividend is cash paid to you.

151
00:10:29,151 --> 00:10:33,014
A buyback is the company buying
its own shares and cancelling them,

152
00:10:33,014 --> 00:10:36,473
so each remaining share owns a
bigger slice of the business.

153
00:10:37,264 --> 00:10:44,477
Visa's share count fell 2.2% a year, which
is the buyback term in the EPS equation.

154
00:10:44,477 --> 00:10:47,345
The dividend yield is only 0.67%.

155
00:10:48,864 --> 00:10:52,241
Chapter five. The time value of
money, and the discount rate.

156
00:10:53,631 --> 00:10:54,755
Now the valuation.

157
00:10:54,755 --> 00:10:59,190
It rests on one idea: a dollar today
is worth more than a dollar later,

158
00:10:59,190 --> 00:11:02,750
because today's dollar can be
invested and earn a return.

159
00:11:03,064 --> 00:11:08,516
At a rate r per year, a dollar grows to
one plus r to the power t after t years.

160
00:11:08,516 --> 00:11:11,583
So a cash flow C received
in t years is worth

161
00:11:11,583 --> 00:11:14,241
C divided by one plus r to the t today.

162
00:11:14,531 --> 00:11:17,562
That's its present value, and
r is called the discount rate.

163
00:11:18,364 --> 00:11:21,081
The discount factor is exponential decay,

164
00:11:21,081 --> 00:11:26,648
e to the minus t times log of one plus r:
the same curve as Newton's law of cooling.

165
00:11:26,964 --> 00:11:31,212
At 8.5%, the half-life is
about eight and a half years:

166
00:11:31,212 --> 00:11:37,623
a dollar due in 2035 is worth about fifty
cents today. At 12% it's about six years.

167
00:11:37,931 --> 00:11:41,738
So the discount rate decides how
much distant cash flows count,

168
00:11:41,738 --> 00:11:45,304
and for Visa, most of the value
lies in the distant future.

169
00:11:46,097 --> 00:11:49,007
Which rate? The discount rate
is your opportunity cost:

170
00:11:49,007 --> 00:11:51,865
the return you could earn
elsewhere for the same risk.

171
00:11:52,164 --> 00:11:54,571
Start from the risk-free rate,

172
00:11:54,571 --> 00:12:00,429
what the US government pays to borrow
for ten years: 5.08% on 16 October.

173
00:12:00,731 --> 00:12:03,626
Shares are riskier than government bonds,

174
00:12:03,626 --> 00:12:08,429
so investors demand an extra return,
called the equity risk premium.

175
00:12:08,429 --> 00:12:13,090
A widely used estimate, from
Professor Aswath Damodaran, is 4.20%.

176
00:12:13,897 --> 00:12:16,646
How much of that premium
applies to Visa depends

177
00:12:16,646 --> 00:12:20,541
on how strongly it moves with the
market, which is measured by beta.

178
00:12:20,831 --> 00:12:25,360
Plot Visa's monthly returns against
the market's and fit a straight line:

179
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:29,145
beta is the slope. Above one,
a stock amplifies market moves.

180
00:12:29,145 --> 00:12:30,697
Below one, it damps them.

181
00:12:30,997 --> 00:12:34,097
Visa's five-year beta is 0.75.

182
00:12:34,897 --> 00:12:38,550
The capital asset pricing
model, or CAPM, combines them:

183
00:12:38,550 --> 00:12:41,550
the required return
equals the risk-free rate,

184
00:12:41,550 --> 00:12:44,158
plus beta times the equity risk premium.

185
00:12:44,464 --> 00:12:52,096
5.08%, plus 0.75 times 4.20%, gives 8.23%.

186
00:12:52,397 --> 00:12:57,992
I round that up to 8.5% for the risks
specific to one company and its regulators.

187
00:12:57,992 --> 00:13:02,965
That's a judgment, and CAPM is a
first-order model with wide error bars.

188
00:13:02,965 --> 00:13:05,797
Chapter seven shows how much they matter.

189
00:13:07,297 --> 00:13:09,862
Chapter six. The
discounted cash flow model.

190
00:13:11,264 --> 00:13:13,712
A discounted cash flow model, or DCF,

191
00:13:13,712 --> 00:13:16,688
says a business is worth
the present value of

192
00:13:16,688 --> 00:13:21,451
all the free cash flow it will ever
produce: the sum, over future years,

193
00:13:21,451 --> 00:13:25,354
of free cash flow in year t
divided by one plus r to the t.

194
00:13:25,664 --> 00:13:27,532
I forecast ten years explicitly,

195
00:13:27,532 --> 00:13:31,734
and treat everything after that as
one block, called the terminal value.

196
00:13:32,531 --> 00:13:39,330
The forecast starts from analysts'
consensus revenue for fiscal 2026, $45.7bn.

197
00:13:39,330 --> 00:13:42,381
It grows 10% a year for five years,

198
00:13:42,381 --> 00:13:47,611
then the growth rate fades in
a straight line to 4% by 2036.

199
00:13:47,931 --> 00:13:53,788
Free cash flow is 51.1%
of revenue every year,

200
00:13:53,788 --> 00:14:00,409
so it rises from $25.7bn
in 2027 to $51.3bn in 2036.

201
00:14:00,697 --> 00:14:02,486
Now discount each year's cash flow,

202
00:14:02,486 --> 00:14:05,808
from the middle of the year, since
cash arrives through the year.

203
00:14:06,097 --> 00:14:14,900
Watch the bars shrink. The $51.3bn
of 2036 is worth only $23.6bn today.

204
00:14:14,900 --> 00:14:20,232
Together, the ten years
are worth $252.5bn.

205
00:14:21,031 --> 00:14:26,198
After 2036, I assume free cash
flow grows 4% a year forever:

206
00:14:26,198 --> 00:14:29,557
about 2% inflation plus 2% real growth.

207
00:14:29,864 --> 00:14:33,900
Each year's cash flow is the
previous one times one plus g,

208
00:14:33,900 --> 00:14:36,910
discounted by one more
factor of one plus r.

209
00:14:36,910 --> 00:14:41,288
That's a geometric series with
ratio one plus g over one plus r.

210
00:14:41,597 --> 00:14:46,926
For r greater than g it converges to next
year's cash flow divided by r minus g:

211
00:14:46,926 --> 00:14:48,658
the Gordon growth formula.

212
00:14:48,658 --> 00:14:53,787
For r less than or equal to g it diverges,
a warning sign we'll come back to.

213
00:14:54,097 --> 00:15:00,768
$51.3bn times 1.04,
divided by 8.5% minus 4%,

214
00:15:00,768 --> 00:15:11,293
gives a terminal value of $1.18
trillion in 2036, worth $545.8bn today.

215
00:15:12,097 --> 00:15:18,256
Together, that's $798bn, the
enterprise value of the whole business.

216
00:15:18,256 --> 00:15:23,238
Subtract net debt, which is
debt minus cash, of $5.2bn,

217
00:15:23,238 --> 00:15:27,404
and divide by the share
count: $419 per share.

218
00:15:27,697 --> 00:15:29,698
That's 4% above today's price.

219
00:15:29,698 --> 00:15:33,166
On these assumptions, Visa
is roughly fairly priced.

220
00:15:33,464 --> 00:15:37,198
But 68% of the value comes
from cash flows after 2036.

221
00:15:37,198 --> 00:15:40,448
Most of what you pay for
is the distant future,

222
00:15:40,448 --> 00:15:43,628
exactly where the discount
rate bites hardest.

223
00:15:45,131 --> 00:15:48,789
Chapter seven. Sensitivity: why
the discount rate dominates.

224
00:15:50,197 --> 00:15:55,441
Start with the terminal value: value
over cash flow equals one over r minus g.

225
00:15:55,441 --> 00:15:59,811
As a function of r, that's a
hyperbola with a pole at r equals g,

226
00:15:59,811 --> 00:16:02,298
like a response curve near resonance.

227
00:16:02,597 --> 00:16:07,384
At r of 8.5% and g of 4%, we
sit 4.5 points from the pole,

228
00:16:07,384 --> 00:16:09,942
where the curve is still steep.

229
00:16:10,231 --> 00:16:15,922
The log-derivative of the value with
respect to r is minus one over r minus g,

230
00:16:15,922 --> 00:16:20,374
about minus 22. So each percentage
point on the discount rate

231
00:16:20,374 --> 00:16:23,292
moves the terminal value by roughly 22%.

232
00:16:23,292 --> 00:16:25,773
Bond investors call this duration.

233
00:16:26,564 --> 00:16:30,556
The full model behaves the same way:

234
00:16:30,556 --> 00:16:36,434
$376 per share at a 9%
discount rate, and $472 at 8%.

235
00:16:36,731 --> 00:16:40,799
Half a point either way, well
inside CAPM's error bars,

236
00:16:40,799 --> 00:16:45,312
moves the answer from 7% below
today's price to 17% above it.

237
00:16:46,131 --> 00:16:49,903
This grid shows the value for
different growth and discount rates.

238
00:16:49,903 --> 00:16:53,046
Blue cells are above today's
price, orange cells below.

239
00:16:53,364 --> 00:16:58,121
The boundary runs diagonally, because higher
growth offsets a higher discount rate,

240
00:16:58,121 --> 00:17:00,127
and the base case sits right on it.

241
00:17:00,931 --> 00:17:07,411
Terminal growth from 3
to 5% gives $357 to $516,

242
00:17:07,411 --> 00:17:15,781
and a free cash flow margin from
45 to 57% gives $368 to $467.

243
00:17:16,097 --> 00:17:21,165
The honest answer: about fair value, with
wide error bars, and no margin of safety.

244
00:17:22,664 --> 00:17:25,954
Chapter eight. The reverse
DCF: what is priced in?

245
00:17:27,364 --> 00:17:30,572
Because the inputs are so
uncertain, flip the question.

246
00:17:30,572 --> 00:17:34,130
Take the price as given, and
solve for the growth it implies.

247
00:17:34,431 --> 00:17:37,176
Value increases smoothly
with the growth rate,

248
00:17:37,176 --> 00:17:40,219
so we need the root of
value of g, minus the price.

249
00:17:41,031 --> 00:17:45,001
The script uses bisection. Start
with a bracket where the value is too

250
00:17:45,001 --> 00:17:47,326
low at one end and too high at the other.

251
00:17:47,631 --> 00:17:51,646
Evaluate the midpoint, keep the half
that contains the root, and repeat.

252
00:17:51,646 --> 00:17:53,319
Each step halves the interval.

253
00:17:53,631 --> 00:18:00,752
The answer: 9.3% a year from fiscal
2027 to 2031, then fading to 4%.

254
00:18:00,752 --> 00:18:04,418
Over ten years, that's 7.7% a year.

255
00:18:05,231 --> 00:18:09,617
Visa's history is 10.2% a
year from fiscal 2017 to 2025.

256
00:18:09,617 --> 00:18:14,237
So the price assumes Visa
roughly repeats its track record:

257
00:18:14,237 --> 00:18:17,840
achievable, but with no
credit for a slowdown.

258
00:18:18,131 --> 00:18:21,653
And again the discount rate dominates.

259
00:18:21,653 --> 00:18:27,122
At 9.5% instead of 8.5%, the
implied growth jumps to 13.0%.

260
00:18:28,628 --> 00:18:31,320
Chapter nine. Multiples,
the market's shorthand.

261
00:18:32,728 --> 00:18:35,670
Investors also use
shortcuts called multiples.

262
00:18:35,670 --> 00:18:38,676
The most common is the
price-to-earnings ratio,

263
00:18:38,676 --> 00:18:41,938
P/E: the share price divided
by earnings per share.

264
00:18:42,228 --> 00:18:44,961
For an engineer, P/E is a payback time:

265
00:18:44,961 --> 00:18:48,744
a P/E of 34 means paying 34
years of today's earnings.

266
00:18:48,744 --> 00:18:54,279
Trailing multiples use the last twelve
months; forward multiples use forecasts.

267
00:18:55,095 --> 00:18:59,611
Visa trades at 34.3 times
trailing GAAP earnings,

268
00:18:59,611 --> 00:19:05,234
31.5 times non-GAAP, and 26.9
times the fiscal 2027 forecast.

269
00:19:05,234 --> 00:19:10,395
The S&P 500 index trades at
19.0 times forward earnings.

270
00:19:11,195 --> 00:19:15,837
Against its own history, Visa is
somewhat expensive but not extreme:

271
00:19:15,837 --> 00:19:20,411
today's P/E is at the 73rd percentile
of its last eleven year-ends.

272
00:19:21,228 --> 00:19:24,828
The comparison with bonds is more telling.

273
00:19:24,828 --> 00:19:31,513
Visa's free cash flow yield, free cash
flow divided by market value, is 2.75%.

274
00:19:31,513 --> 00:19:34,513
The ten-year Treasury yields 5.08%.

275
00:19:34,828 --> 00:19:39,798
Until 2022, Visa's cash yield was
above the Treasury yield every year.

276
00:19:39,798 --> 00:19:44,769
Today it is 2.3 percentage points
below, the widest gap in the series.

277
00:19:45,561 --> 00:19:50,081
Why accept less than a risk-free bond?
Because you expect growth. Rearranged,

278
00:19:50,081 --> 00:19:54,660
the Gordon formula says the return equals
the cash yield plus the growth rate.

279
00:19:54,961 --> 00:20:00,984
With an 8.5% required return and a
2.9% yield on next year's cash flow,

280
00:20:00,984 --> 00:20:04,122
the price needs 5.6% growth, forever.

281
00:20:04,122 --> 00:20:07,939
The multiples tell the
same story as the DCF.

282
00:20:09,428 --> 00:20:12,412
Chapter ten. Scenarios
and the expected return.

283
00:20:13,828 --> 00:20:17,130
A valuation gives a number,
but you need a return.

284
00:20:17,130 --> 00:20:21,093
The standard measure is the
internal rate of return, or IRR.

285
00:20:21,395 --> 00:20:23,872
On a timeline, you pay the price today,

286
00:20:23,872 --> 00:20:26,983
receive dividends each
year, and sell at the end.

287
00:20:26,983 --> 00:20:29,714
The IRR is the discount
rate that makes the

288
00:20:29,714 --> 00:20:34,350
net present value of these cash flows
zero: another root-finding problem.

289
00:20:35,161 --> 00:20:37,284
To forecast the cash flows,

290
00:20:37,284 --> 00:20:42,630
start from analysts' forecast of
earnings per share for fiscal 2027,

291
00:20:42,630 --> 00:20:45,696
$14.94, and grow it at a scenario rate.

292
00:20:45,995 --> 00:20:50,269
The exit price is the earnings at
that time, times a P/E multiple,

293
00:20:50,269 --> 00:20:54,283
and along the way you collect
dividends at today's 21% payout.

294
00:20:54,283 --> 00:20:58,946
So two levers decide the return:
earnings growth, and the exit multiple.

295
00:20:59,761 --> 00:21:05,207
In the bear case, at 25%, US routing
rules or a court remedy bite,

296
00:21:05,207 --> 00:21:09,414
rival payment systems
spread, and a recession hits.

297
00:21:09,414 --> 00:21:13,787
Earnings grow 5% a year and
the multiple falls to 18:

298
00:21:13,787 --> 00:21:17,417
$343 after five years,
or minus 2.2% a year.

299
00:21:17,728 --> 00:21:25,604
In the base case, at 50%, growth fades
gradually and the multiple drifts down to 24.

300
00:21:25,604 --> 00:21:30,011
Earnings grow 11% a year:
$604, or 9.3% a year.

301
00:21:30,328 --> 00:21:37,475
In the bull case, at 25%, new businesses
accelerate and regulation is benign.

302
00:21:37,475 --> 00:21:43,786
Earnings grow 14% a year at a
multiple of 28: $805, or 15.6% a year.

303
00:21:44,095 --> 00:21:46,637
The probabilities are
my judgment, not data.

304
00:21:47,428 --> 00:21:51,673
Weight each scenario's ending
wealth by its probability,

305
00:21:51,673 --> 00:21:57,586
and convert to an annual rate: 8.6% a
year over five years, and 8.4% over ten.

306
00:21:57,895 --> 00:22:01,536
Averaging the three returns
instead would give about 8.0%.

307
00:22:01,536 --> 00:22:04,612
Compounding is convex, so
by Jensen's inequality,

308
00:22:04,612 --> 00:22:08,880
the average of the rates understates
the rate of the average wealth.

309
00:22:09,695 --> 00:22:12,882
This grid shows the five-year
return for any combination

310
00:22:12,882 --> 00:22:14,988
of earnings growth and exit multiple.

311
00:22:15,295 --> 00:22:20,552
A double-digit return needs 11%
growth with a multiple of 26,

312
00:22:20,552 --> 00:22:24,171
13% with 24, or 15% with 22. All possible,

313
00:22:24,171 --> 00:22:28,221
but the median outcome
looks more like 9 to 11%

314
00:22:28,221 --> 00:22:32,272
growth and a multiple
drifting toward 22 to 24.

315
00:22:33,061 --> 00:22:38,314
For the index fund, the market-implied
expected return is the risk-free

316
00:22:38,314 --> 00:22:41,717
rate plus the equity
risk premium, about 9.3%.

317
00:22:41,717 --> 00:22:44,898
A more conservative
estimate, based on J.P.

318
00:22:44,898 --> 00:22:47,265
Morgan's forecasts, is about 7%.

319
00:22:47,561 --> 00:22:51,264
Visa's 8.4 to 8.6% sits inside that range,

320
00:22:51,264 --> 00:22:55,583
consistent with its CAPM
cost of capital of 8.2%.

321
00:22:55,583 --> 00:22:59,814
That suggests the market
is pricing Visa fairly.

322
00:23:00,628 --> 00:23:04,809
The lower the price you pay, the higher
the expected return, along this curve.

323
00:23:05,128 --> 00:23:11,705
A 10% expected return over ten
years, 1 to 3 points above the index,

324
00:23:11,705 --> 00:23:17,122
needs a price of about $346,
23 times forecast earnings.

325
00:23:17,122 --> 00:23:21,668
Visa was at $341 as
recently as September 2025.

326
00:23:23,161 --> 00:23:25,086
Chapter eleven. What could go wrong.

327
00:23:26,495 --> 00:23:28,633
Now the bear case, taken seriously.

328
00:23:28,633 --> 00:23:31,933
Visa's moat is under slow
attack from four directions.

329
00:23:32,228 --> 00:23:34,866
First, and most important,
the United States.

330
00:23:34,866 --> 00:23:38,442
Rules that let merchants choose
how card payments are routed,

331
00:23:38,442 --> 00:23:40,670
or steer customers to cheaper options,

332
00:23:40,670 --> 00:23:43,602
would shift the choice of
network to the merchant.

333
00:23:43,895 --> 00:23:46,911
And large banks can take volume in-house.

334
00:23:46,911 --> 00:23:50,516
Capital One, which now
owns the Discover network,

335
00:23:50,516 --> 00:23:54,636
moved about 25 million debit
cards onto it in July 2026.

336
00:23:54,928 --> 00:23:58,258
Second, government-backed
instant payments.

337
00:23:58,258 --> 00:24:04,762
In Brazil, Pix took about 42% of online
purchases in 2025, edging past credit cards.

338
00:24:04,762 --> 00:24:10,027
India's UPI handled 24 billion
transactions in September 2026 alone,

339
00:24:10,027 --> 00:24:13,047
and Europe is building its own systems.

340
00:24:13,361 --> 00:24:17,997
Third, stablecoins in cross-border
payments, Visa's richest line. Fourth,

341
00:24:17,997 --> 00:24:22,951
wallets and AI agents that pick the
cheapest payment method in the background.

342
00:24:23,761 --> 00:24:25,639
Regulators are pressing too.

343
00:24:25,639 --> 00:24:29,864
A settlement of the big US merchant
lawsuit would let merchants

344
00:24:29,864 --> 00:24:32,948
add surcharges and decline
some premium cards.

345
00:24:32,948 --> 00:24:35,362
It got preliminary approval in June,

346
00:24:35,362 --> 00:24:38,983
and the fairness hearing is
reportedly on 16 November.

347
00:24:39,295 --> 00:24:41,344
The Credit Card Competition Act,

348
00:24:41,344 --> 00:24:44,739
which would force a second
network onto credit cards,

349
00:24:44,739 --> 00:24:46,788
has the President's endorsement.

350
00:24:46,788 --> 00:24:52,104
The Justice Department is suing Visa over
debit, with a trial expected around 2028.

351
00:24:52,104 --> 00:24:54,794
The UK and the EU are looking at fees too.

352
00:24:55,095 --> 00:24:56,976
History offers some comfort.

353
00:24:56,976 --> 00:25:02,483
When US debit fees were capped in 2011,
Visa's shares rose more than 15% in a day,

354
00:25:02,483 --> 00:25:05,573
because the final rule
was looser than feared.

355
00:25:05,573 --> 00:25:10,409
Regulation has repeatedly hurt the
multiple more than long-run earnings.

356
00:25:11,228 --> 00:25:14,919
The economy adds near-term
risk: weak job growth,

357
00:25:14,919 --> 00:25:19,138
inflation at 3.7%, and a Fed
that has just raised rates.

358
00:25:19,138 --> 00:25:22,604
Revenue would probably
hold up in a recession,

359
00:25:22,604 --> 00:25:27,952
but the stock would still fall 30%
or more, as it did in 2008 and 2020.

360
00:25:28,761 --> 00:25:33,041
The report's red-team section
tested ten arguments against buying.

361
00:25:33,041 --> 00:25:37,515
Four survive scrutiny: a growth
multiple in a 5% interest-rate world,

362
00:25:37,515 --> 00:25:40,108
big banks learning to route around Visa,

363
00:25:40,108 --> 00:25:43,934
state payment systems capturing
the emerging-market growth,

364
00:25:43,934 --> 00:25:46,333
and base rates that favour the index.

365
00:25:46,628 --> 00:25:49,243
None of them says Visa is a bad business.

366
00:25:49,243 --> 00:25:51,219
Together they say that at $402,

367
00:25:51,219 --> 00:25:54,471
you're paying full price
for a great business whose

368
00:25:54,471 --> 00:25:57,596
growth is more likely to
slow down than speed up.

369
00:25:59,095 --> 00:26:01,922
Chapter twelve.
Diversification and base rates.

370
00:26:03,328 --> 00:26:06,693
Why does it matter that Visa
offers about the index's return?

371
00:26:06,693 --> 00:26:09,781
Because of what happens to
risk when you combine stocks.

372
00:26:10,095 --> 00:26:14,560
Split each stock's risk into market
risk, which moves all stocks together,

373
00:26:14,560 --> 00:26:17,818
and company-specific risk,
which is independent noise.

374
00:26:18,128 --> 00:26:21,344
In a portfolio of N stocks,
the market part stays,

375
00:26:21,344 --> 00:26:25,075
but the variance of the specific
part falls as one over N,

376
00:26:25,075 --> 00:26:28,484
just like averaging N
independent noisy measurements.

377
00:26:28,795 --> 00:26:31,307
A global index holds thousands of stocks,

378
00:26:31,307 --> 00:26:34,310
so its company-specific
risk is practically gone.

379
00:26:34,310 --> 00:26:36,210
A single stock keeps all of it.

380
00:26:37,028 --> 00:26:40,494
And in CAPM, the market pays
you only for market risk.

381
00:26:40,494 --> 00:26:44,023
Company-specific risk can be
diversified away for free,

382
00:26:44,023 --> 00:26:46,013
so nobody pays you to carry it.

383
00:26:46,013 --> 00:26:51,018
A single stock is only worth holding if
its expected return is clearly higher,

384
00:26:51,018 --> 00:26:52,109
and Visa's isn't.

385
00:26:52,928 --> 00:26:58,060
The base rates are sobering. Studying
all US stocks from 1926 to 2016,

386
00:26:58,060 --> 00:27:02,239
Hendrik Bessembinder found
that just 4.3% of them created

387
00:27:02,239 --> 00:27:05,759
all of the market's wealth
above Treasury bills.

388
00:27:06,561 --> 00:27:11,414
Single-stock returns are extremely skewed:
a few huge winners pull up the average,

389
00:27:11,414 --> 00:27:14,314
so the typical stock does
worse than the average.

390
00:27:14,628 --> 00:27:19,326
Professionals struggle too.
Over the 20 years to 2025,

391
00:27:19,326 --> 00:27:25,328
about 93% of actively managed US
large-cap funds trailed the S&P 500.

392
00:27:26,128 --> 00:27:28,925
Visa has been one of the rare winners,

393
00:27:28,925 --> 00:27:33,267
returning about 18% a year in
dollars since September 2015,

394
00:27:33,267 --> 00:27:36,874
against about 13% for the
S&P 500 on price alone.

395
00:27:36,874 --> 00:27:40,554
But past winners staying
winners is the exception.

396
00:27:40,861 --> 00:27:48,348
And you already own Visa through the index
fund, at about 0.57% of the global index.

397
00:27:48,348 --> 00:27:53,072
40,000 kroner in Visa, in
a 500,000 kroner portfolio,

398
00:27:53,072 --> 00:27:57,975
would raise your Visa exposure
from about 0.6% to 8.5%.

399
00:27:59,461 --> 00:28:04,040
Chapter thirteen. The Norwegian angle:
currency, tax and position size.

400
00:28:05,428 --> 00:28:08,561
You invest in kroner,
so the currency matters.

401
00:28:08,561 --> 00:28:13,874
If Visa returns r in dollars, and the
dollar rises by delta against the krone,

402
00:28:13,874 --> 00:28:18,574
your return in kroner is one plus
r, times one plus delta, minus one.

403
00:28:18,574 --> 00:28:20,550
In log terms they simply add.

404
00:28:21,361 --> 00:28:28,059
On 9 October a dollar cost 9.57 kroner,
above the ten-year average of 9.39,

405
00:28:28,059 --> 00:28:32,256
and well above the
twenty-year average of 7.86.

406
00:28:32,561 --> 00:28:38,110
Usefully, the krone tends to weaken
when stock markets fall. Since 2000,

407
00:28:38,110 --> 00:28:41,731
the monthly correlation
between the S&P 500 and

408
00:28:41,731 --> 00:28:44,968
the dollar-krone rate has been minus 0.35.

409
00:28:45,761 --> 00:28:52,500
In the three big sell-offs, the dollar
rose 39, 22 and 23% against the krone.

410
00:28:52,500 --> 00:28:58,188
So Visa's 52% fall in 2008 and
2009 was only about 33% in kroner.

411
00:28:58,188 --> 00:29:02,301
Dollar exposure works
like a damper on crashes.

412
00:29:02,595 --> 00:29:04,752
The long-run risk runs the other way:

413
00:29:04,752 --> 00:29:07,783
a return to the twenty-year
average would cost about

414
00:29:07,783 --> 00:29:10,348
two percentage points
a year over ten years.

415
00:29:11,161 --> 00:29:16,102
The five-year expected
return of 8.6% becomes 6.1%

416
00:29:16,102 --> 00:29:20,450
if the krone strengthens
to 8.50 per dollar,

417
00:29:20,450 --> 00:29:23,710
and 10.7% if it weakens to 10.50.

418
00:29:24,028 --> 00:29:27,878
But your global index fund
is about 64% US stocks too,

419
00:29:27,878 --> 00:29:31,870
so currency isn't a reason
to prefer one over the other.

420
00:29:32,661 --> 00:29:36,103
Now tax. The Norwegian share
savings account, the ASK,

421
00:29:36,103 --> 00:29:40,818
lets you buy and sell without paying
tax on gains until you withdraw them.

422
00:29:41,128 --> 00:29:46,497
But it only accepts shares in companies
resident in the European Economic Area,

423
00:29:46,497 --> 00:29:50,847
plus equity funds. Visa is not
eligible. A global index fund is.

424
00:29:51,661 --> 00:29:55,036
Share income, both gains and dividends,

425
00:29:55,036 --> 00:30:00,919
is multiplied by 1.72 and taxed at
22%: an effective rate of 37.84%.

426
00:30:00,919 --> 00:30:07,755
Gains are calculated in kroner, so currency
moves are part of the taxable gain.

427
00:30:08,061 --> 00:30:13,992
The US withholds 15% of dividends if
you file a W-8BEN form with your broker,

428
00:30:13,992 --> 00:30:19,076
otherwise 30%, and Norway credits
that against your Norwegian tax.

429
00:30:19,395 --> 00:30:24,505
A shielding deduction exempts a small
yearly return on your purchase price.

430
00:30:24,505 --> 00:30:28,662
Visa's dividend is so small that
the deduction can absorb it,

431
00:30:28,662 --> 00:30:33,636
leaving the US tax uncredited: about
600 kroner over ten years on 40,000.

432
00:30:33,636 --> 00:30:35,408
Small, but worth checking.

433
00:30:36,195 --> 00:30:42,729
Put together: 40,000 kroner held for
ten years at a flat exchange rate

434
00:30:42,729 --> 00:30:47,116
grows to about 73,800
kroner after tax in Visa,

435
00:30:47,116 --> 00:30:51,877
and to 67,700 to 79,000 in
an index fund in an ASK.

436
00:30:52,195 --> 00:30:57,146
Tax barely separates them. The real
advantage of the ASK is flexibility:

437
00:30:57,146 --> 00:31:00,653
you can switch or rebalance
without triggering tax.

438
00:31:01,461 --> 00:31:02,458
Finally, size.

439
00:31:02,458 --> 00:31:07,936
A common rule of thumb is at most 5% of
your investments in any single stock.

440
00:31:07,936 --> 00:31:11,494
30 to 50 thousand kroner
fits if your portfolio is

441
00:31:11,494 --> 00:31:14,838
about 600 thousand to a
million kroner or more.

442
00:31:16,328 --> 00:31:19,228
Chapter fourteen. The
conclusion, and what to watch.

443
00:31:20,628 --> 00:31:22,031
Back to the three questions.

444
00:31:22,328 --> 00:31:24,211
Is Visa a great business?

445
00:31:24,211 --> 00:31:30,009
Yes, with high confidence: a toll on
global spending, with margins above 60%,

446
00:31:30,009 --> 00:31:34,527
almost no capital needs, and
revenue that grew even in 2009.

447
00:31:34,828 --> 00:31:39,312
Is the price attractive? No. It
assumes Visa repeats its record,

448
00:31:39,312 --> 00:31:44,568
and its expected return of about 8.5%
a year is no better than the index's.

449
00:31:44,861 --> 00:31:48,297
So is it better than the index
fund for you? Not at this price.

450
00:31:48,297 --> 00:31:51,460
You'd take on concentrated
risk without being paid for it.

451
00:31:52,261 --> 00:31:55,927
The recommendation is hold, or
wait, with medium confidence.

452
00:31:55,927 --> 00:31:58,432
Keep the money in the index fund for now.

453
00:31:58,728 --> 00:32:05,711
Become a buyer at or below about $345,
roughly 23 times next year's earnings.

454
00:32:05,711 --> 00:32:12,513
Between $345 and $380, a starter position
of at most a third is reasonable,

455
00:32:12,513 --> 00:32:14,690
ideally bought in steps.

456
00:32:15,495 --> 00:32:19,038
Revisit after the full-year
results on 27 October,

457
00:32:19,038 --> 00:32:21,660
and the court hearing on 16 November.

458
00:32:21,961 --> 00:32:25,154
Confidence is medium
because the answer hinges

459
00:32:25,154 --> 00:32:27,862
on a discount rate nobody can pin down.

460
00:32:27,862 --> 00:32:30,013
At 8%, the model would say buy.

461
00:32:30,828 --> 00:32:33,897
Five signals would tell you the
investment case is breaking.

462
00:32:34,195 --> 00:32:38,775
One: client incentives above 30% of
gross revenue while growth slows.

463
00:32:39,095 --> 00:32:43,318
Two: Visa's US volume growing clearly
slower than consumer spending,

464
00:32:43,318 --> 00:32:47,045
or more banks following Capital
One onto their own networks.

465
00:32:47,361 --> 00:32:52,090
Three: regulation, such as a Senate
vote on the Credit Card Competition Act,

466
00:32:52,090 --> 00:32:56,694
a court remedy in the debit case, or
merchants actually adding surcharges.

467
00:32:56,995 --> 00:33:01,009
Four: account-to-account payments
winning in Visa's growth markets,

468
00:33:01,009 --> 00:33:04,245
such as Pix passing half of
Brazilian online shopping.

469
00:33:04,561 --> 00:33:09,495
Five: revenue growth below 8% in
constant currency, outside a recession.

470
00:33:10,295 --> 00:33:14,025
Every number here comes from the
report's data and Python scripts,

471
00:33:14,025 --> 00:33:16,624
which you can rerun with
your own assumptions.

472
00:33:16,624 --> 00:33:19,902
Change the discount rate, the
growth or the probabilities,

473
00:33:19,902 --> 00:33:21,767
and see how the conclusion moves.

474
00:33:22,561 --> 00:33:27,266
Thanks for watching. Remember, this is
an analysis to support your decision,

475
00:33:27,266 --> 00:33:29,123
not personal financial advice.

